SUMMARY:Andy Smith\, tuba/euphonium\; Tomoko Kashiwagi\, piano
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  Shostakovich/Atovmyan/McCourt: 'Waltz No. 2' from
  "Suite for variety orchestra" (c.1956?) Allen Vizzutti: Cascades (1980/2
 009)R. Schumann: Drei Romanzen\, Op. 94 (1849)H. Eccles: Violin Sonata in
  G Minor (1720)Koetsier: Concertino\, Op. 77 (1978/82)About the ArtistsAn
 dy Smith was appointed by to the Lyric Opera of Chicago orchestra by Andr
 ew Davis in January of 2012. He began performing with the group in Septem
 ber of that same year. Smith\, from Amarillo\, Texas\, earned a Bachelor 
 of Music degree from Texas Tech University\, where he studied with Kevin 
 Wass. He has also studied at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Musi
 c with Daniel Perantoni. Prior to winning the Lyric Opera position\, Smit
 h was a highly sought after tubist in Texas\, where he performed regularl
 y with the Lubbock and Big Spring Symphonies. In addition to his duties i
 n the pit\, Smith has served as the principal tubist for the Grant Park O
 rchestra since 2018. He has also performed with many different orchestras
  in the Midwest including the Chicago Symphony Orchestra\, the Detroit Sy
 mphony Orchestra\, the St. Louis Symphony Orchestra\, the Milwaukee Symph
 ony Orchestra\, and the Indianapolis Symphony Orchestra. Smith is a Buffe
 t Performing Artist. Smith is on the faculty of the DePaul School of Musi
 c.Tomoko Kashiwagi is visiting lecturer in music in chamber and collabora
 tive music at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music. Kashiwagi wa
 s previously associate professor of piano and collaborative piano and hel
 d the Emily J. McAllister Endowed Chair at the University of Arkansas\, w
 here she joined the faculty in 2012. She is also the founder of the Ozark
  Music Initiative\, a nonprofit dedicated to building community through m
 usic. Her work has been supported by multiple Mid-America Arts Alliance g
 rants. In 2025\, she was inducted into the Steinway &amp\; Sons Music Tea
 cher Hall of Fame in recognition of her commitment and passion as piano e
 ducator. She has presented multiple times at CollabFest\, the conference 
 of the International Keyboard Collaborative Arts Society. Maintaining an 
 active performance career across the United States and internationally\, 
 Kashiwagi is dedicated to exploring a wide range of repertoire as both a 
 performer and educator\, with a particular passion for collaborative play
 ing. She earned a Bachelor of Music\, Master of Music\, and Performer Dip
 loma in Piano Performance from Indiana University. She later became the i
 naugural recipient of the Doctor of Musical Arts in Collaborative Piano d
 egree from the University of Texas at Austin\, where she studied with Ann
 e Epperson\, current chair of Jacobs' Department of Chamber and Collabora
 tive Music.
