BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20260913T220000Z
DTEND:20260913T233000Z
LOCATION:Switchyard Park Pavilion
SUMMARY:Symphony Orchestra – Fawzi Haimor\, guest conductor
DESCRIPTION:Repertoire  Buck: Festival Overture (on the American National
  Air\, "The Star-Spangled Banner") (1879)Ginastera: Danzas del ballet Est
 ancia\, Op. 8a (1941)Dvořák: Symphony No. 9 in E Minor ("From the New W
 orld")\, Op. 95 (1893)About the Artist  Now in his second season as music
  director of the Marin Symphony\, Fawzi Haimor leads the orchestra in cel
 ebration of its 75-year history. Over the course of the 2026-27 season\, 
 Haimor continues to conduct a compelling range of repertoire\, from maste
 rworks by Mahler\, Berlioz\, Bernstein\, and Gershwin to music by living 
 composers including John Williams\, Kareem Roustom\, Gabriela Ortiz\, and
  Reza Vali\, and works by the long-underrecognized Florence Price\, refle
 cting his deep commitment to both tradition and innovation. Praised for h
 is "inspiring conducting\," "sensitivity and his ability to create a defi
 ned\, transparent sound"\, and his "sense of drama and particular gift fo
 r strong\, emphatic finishes\," Haimor has led distinguished orchestras t
 hroughout the United States and around the world\, including the National
  Symphony Orchestra\, Indianapolis Symphony\, New Zealand Symphony Orches
 tra\, BBC Symphony Orchestra\, BBC Philharmonic Orchestra\, Pittsburgh Sy
 mphony\, and the Calgary Philharmonic. Upcoming engagements include a ret
 urn to the Chineke! Orchestra at London's Royal Festival Hall in a progra
 m featuring works by Valerie Coleman and the London premiere of Laura and
  Sarah Ayoub's Arabian Symphony\, as well as appearances with the Alabama
  Symphony Orchestra and at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Music.
  Haimor served as music director of Germany's Württembergische Philharmo
 nie Reutlingen from 2017 to 2020\, leading the orchestra at their home ve
 nue\, Stadthalle Reutlingen\, and on tour throughout Germany and Europe. 
 During his tenure\, Haimor recorded three albums for CPO Records\, includ
 ing orchestral works of George Antheil\, the piano concertos of Eduard Fr
 anck\, and Furtwängler's Symphony No. 1\, and collaborated with the Lond
 on-based Chineke! Orchestra on a recording of works by Coleridge-Taylor f
 or Decca. Haimor's extensive international engagements include performanc
 es with the Orchestre Philharmonique du Luxembourg\, Orchestre de Chambre
  de Paris\, NDR Radiophilharmonie Hannover\, Deutsche Radiophilharmonie K
 aiserslautern\, Orquestra Sinfónica do Porto Casa da Música\, Oulu Sinf
 onia\, Orchestra Sinfonica di Milano Giuseppe Verdi\, Odense Symphony\, K
 yoto Symphony\, New Zealand Symphony\, and Qatar Philharmonic\, among oth
 ers.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Repertoire</strong><br />  Buck: 
 <em>Festival Overture (on the American National Air\, "The Star-Spangled 
 Banner") (1879)<br /></em>Ginastera: <em>Danzas del ballet Estancia\, Op.
  8a (1941)<br /></em>Dvořák: <em>Symphony No. 9 in E Minor ("From the N
 ew World")\, Op. 95 (1893)<br /></em><br /><strong>About the Artist</stro
 ng><br /><br />  Now in his second season as music director of the Marin 
 Symphony\, <strong>Fawzi Haimor</strong> leads the orchestra in celebrati
 on of its 75-year history. Over the course of the 2026-27 season\, Haimor
  continues to conduct a compelling range of repertoire\, from masterworks
  by Mahler\, Berlioz\, Bernstein\, and Gershwin to music by living compos
 ers including John Williams\, Kareem Roustom\, Gabriela Ortiz\, and Reza 
 Vali\, and works by the long-underrecognized Florence Price\, reflecting 
 his deep commitment to both tradition and innovation. Praised for his "in
 spiring conducting\," "sensitivity and his ability to create a defined\, 
 transparent sound"\, and his "sense of drama and particular gift for stro
 ng\, emphatic finishes\," Haimor has led distinguished orchestras through
 out the United States and around the world\, including the National Symph
 ony Orchestra\, Indianapolis Symphony\, New Zealand Symphony Orchestra\, 
 BBC Symphony Orchestra\, BBC Philharmonic Orchestra\, Pittsburgh Symphony
 \, and the Calgary Philharmonic. Upcoming engagements include a return to
  the Chineke! Orchestra at London's Royal Festival Hall in a program feat
 uring works by Valerie Coleman and the London premiere of Laura and Sarah
  Ayoub's <em>Arabian Symphony</em>\, as well as appearances with the Alab
 ama Symphony Orchestra and at the Indiana University Jacobs School of Mus
 ic. Haimor served as music director of Germany's Württembergische Philha
 rmonie Reutlingen from 2017 to 2020\, leading the orchestra at their home
  venue\, Stadthalle Reutlingen\, and on tour throughout Germany and Europ
 e. During his tenure\, Haimor recorded three albums for CPO Records\, inc
 luding orchestral works of George Antheil\, the piano concertos of Eduard
  Franck\, and Furtwängler's Symphony No. 1\, and collaborated with the L
 ondon-based Chineke! Orchestra on a recording of works by Coleridge-Taylo
 r for Decca. Haimor's extensive international engagements include perform
 ances with the Orchestre Philharmonique du Luxembourg\, Orchestre de Cham
 bre de Paris\, NDR Radiophilharmonie Hannover\, Deutsche Radiophilharmoni
 e Kaiserslautern\, Orquestra Sinfónica do Porto Casa da Música\, Oulu S
 infonia\, Orchestra Sinfonica di Milano Giuseppe Verdi\, Odense Symphony\
 , Kyoto Symphony\, New Zealand Symphony\, and Qatar Philharmonic\, among 
 others.</p>
